    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Steamboats navigating the Arkansas River starting in the 1820s delivered supplies and goods to outposts like Fort Gibson near River Bottom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/river_bottom_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "River Bottom formed part of the Muscogee Creek Nation within Indian Territory during the late nineteenth century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/river_bottom_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Creek Freedmen resettled in the river bottoms after 1866 to establish farms using the rich alluvial soils for cotton production.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/river_bottom_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Dawes Commission administered land surveys in the late 1890s, allotting 160-acre parcels to individual Creek citizens in the river valley.",
